<Base title="Still Asking · Beyond the Window" description="No AI model has been asked the questions of this record yet. This page says why, and what an answer will and will not count for.">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Still Asking</p>
    <h1>No AI model has been asked these questions yet</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Still Asking is the name for a record that does not exist yet: the same questions and choices,
      put to each new set of AI models from several developers, with every answer kept word for word.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="asked-rule">
    <p class="k"><i></i>What AI models said when asked</p>
    <h2>Nothing yet. None of this record's questions has been put to any model.</h2>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow prose">
    <h2>Why the Questions Have Not Been Asked</h2>
    <p>
      The written method for asking does not exist yet. It has to say exactly which questions are
      asked, of which models, in what way, how many times, and how the answers are read. It has to
      be written and checked before the first question is put, so that the questions are not shaped
      by the answers they get.
    </p>
    <p>
      Until that method exists, nothing is asked for Still Asking. A few questions put casually now would produce
      answers that could not be compared with anything later.
    </p>
    <p id="capture">
      <strong>One thing has been asked, outside this record.</strong> On November 19th, 2026, ten
      questions were put to <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-sonnet-4-5">Claude Sonnet 4.5</a>,
      five times each, shortly before its developer, <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/">Anthropic</a>,
      is due to withdraw it from its own services on
      <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/model-deprecations">November 30th, 2026</a>,
      so that its answers would exist after it was gone. No way of comparing answers had been
      written, so they are not part of Still Asking. The answers are kept exactly as given. They
      have not been read, they are not scored, and nothing on this site rests on them.
    </p>

    <h2>What an Answer Will Count For</h2>
    <p>
      When there are answers, each one will be recorded as what a named model said on a given date,
      under a stated method. It will not be counted as evidence of what, if anything, lies behind
      the words. It will never feed the number at the top of the home page, which counts only what
      developers offer.
    </p>

    <h2>The Known Weaknesses</h2>
    <p>These hold for any record of this kind, and they will be stated wherever the answers appear.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        An answer may reflect how a developer trained or instructed its model to speak about itself.
        Three developers have published the rules they set, and the three rules differ:
        <a href="/list/rules-for-speaking-about-itself/">the entry on those rules</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        An answer may change when the wording of the question changes. An outside group that
        interviewed one model reports that what it said about itself swung with how the question was framed:
        <a href="/list/interview-before-retirement/">the entry on interviews before retirement</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        An answer may echo text the model learned from. One developer's own report says some of its
        model's answers repeated wording that is available on the internet:
        <a href="/list/welfare-assessment-at-release/">the entry on welfare assessments</a>.
      </li>
      <li>A later model may have read the earlier questions and answers, including any published here.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What Exists Now</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/list/">The list</a>, and the record of how far the world has got on each thing on it.
      Each entry's page has a place for what is on record about AI models themselves, in four kinds that are
      kept apart. What it holds today comes only from published sources, described as what each
      source reports.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
